import type { TranslationTable } from "@/lib/translations";
import { PORTUGUESE_ADVANCED_BY_GERMAN } from "@/lib/portugueseAdvancedTranslations";

/**
 * European Portuguese translations keyed by the German catalogue text.
 *
 * Portuguese starts as a narrowed course: only entries present in this table
 * are exposed. That keeps every visible card genuinely Portuguese while the
 * table grows, instead of ever falling back to German under a Portuguese flag.
 *
 * THE VARIETY IS EUROPEAN, THROUGHOUT. Not a preference — a rule the whole
 * table has to keep, because half of it in one variety and half in the other
 * teaches a learner to switch mid-sentence. So: comboio and not trem,
 * autocarro and not ónibus, pequeno-almoço and not café da manhã, telemóvel
 * and not celular, casa de banho and not banheiro, ecrã and not tela, ficheiro
 * and not arquivo, sumo and not suco, gelado and not sorvete, rapariga and not
 * menina, cão and not cachorro, castanho and not marrom, dezasseis and not
 * dezesseis. Some of these differ by more than taste: constipação is a head
 * cold in Portugal and something else entirely in Brazil, and someone who
 * catches one in Lisbon needs the Lisbon word.
 *
 * Spelling follows the orthographic agreement as Portugal applies it — aspeto,
 * eletricidade, projeto, exceção — including the months in lower case.
 *
 * THE STANDARD is what a Portuguese speaker would actually say in that
 * situation, at the register the German uses. A German sentence pattern
 * rebuilt word for word fails even when every word is right, because the
 * learner cannot tell and will repeat it.
 *   - hinkriegen → desenrascar, the word Portuguese already has for sorting
 *     something out by improvising, rather than a neutral conseguir.
 *   - Sperrmüll → os monos, what the council collection is actually called.
 *   - auswendig → de cor. Saber de cor is the fixed phrase.
 *   - hoffentlich → oxalá, which carries the hoping instead of reporting it.
 *
 * NOUNS CARRY THEIR ARTICLE, because Portuguese gender is not guessable from
 * the German and a bare form drops half the word. Keys may be written bare:
 * translate() strips a leading der/die/das before its second lookup, so
 * "Haus" answers for "das Haus" and one key serves both.
 *
 * FORMAL ADDRESS IS o senhor / a senhora, following the German — du is tu,
 * Sie is o senhor. The masculine is written where a card can show only one.
 *
 * WHERE TWO GERMAN CARDS MEET ONE PORTUGUESE WORD they are left to collide and
 * the course keeps one card carrying both meanings. Portuguese really has one
 * word for machen and tun, for öffnen and aufmachen, for the evening and the
 * night. Inventing a second to keep the card count up would teach one nobody
 * says.
 */
