  // ── Election day: the ballot paper, the booth, the two votes (part359) ──
  //
  // Portugal votes with the same furniture and names all of it: a secção de
  // voto, o boletim de voto, a câmara de voto, a urna, o membro da mesa,
  // a afluência às urnas. The counting is by hand in both countries and the
  // cake in the foyer needs no explaining.
  //
  // The two votes do not exist in Portugal, which elects by list alone. So
  // they are numbered rather than named — o primeiro voto, o segundo voto —
  // and the cards beside them say what each one does. The Bundestag keeps its
  // name, because that is the parliament whose seats the second vote decides.
