  // ── Swearing and insults (part29) ───────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // The pack's real subject is where the line is: swearing at a situation is
  // ordinary, insulting a person is a criminal offence in Germany, and the
  // cards say so. So the strength has to survive the crossing. Softening merda
  // to bolas everywhere would teach a learner to hear an insult as a mild
  // complaint, which is the mistake that gets somebody hurt.
  //
  // SEVEN CARDS OF THIS PACK ARE LEFT OUT and the build refuses them. They are
  // the ones explaining that the German word Schwanz means both an animal's
  // tail and, of a man, something vulgar. No Portuguese word carries both, so
  // there is nothing for the lesson to be about — see the list in
  // check-portuguese-table.cjs. The idioms built on it stay, because Portugal
  // has its own: den Schwanz einziehen is meter o rabo entre as pernas, and da
  // war kein Schwanz is não estava lá vivalma.
